A @code{for} loop can also iterate over the contents of lists, general expressions, and hash tables, as follows.

@example
for @var{variable} in @var{list} @var{end_tests} do @var{body}

for @var{variable} in @var{expr} @var{end_tests} do @var{body}

for @var{variable} in @var{hash_table} @var{end_tests} do @var{body}
@end example

In each case,
@var{body} is evaluated once for @var{variable} assigned
each element of @var{list},
each argument of @var{expr},
or each key-value pair of @var{hash_table},
respectively.
If present,
@var{end_tests} terminate the loop if any test evaluates to @code{true};
otherwise the loop terminates when the elements of @var{list},
the arguments of @var{expr},
or the key-value pairs of @var{hash_table}
are exhausted,
or when @code{return} is executed in @var{body}.

@var{hash_table} may be an undeclared array,
either as a symbol property (created with @code{use_fast_arrays} equal to @code{false})
or a value (created with @code{use_fast_arrays} equal to @code{true}),
or a hash table created by @code{make_array(hashed, ...)}.

Values are assigned to @var{variable} using the general assignment operator @code{":"}.
Therefore any assignment to a symbol (not a subscripted expression) which is possible via @code{":"}
is also permissible for iterating over the contents of expressions.
In particular,
destructuring assignments are recognized;
these may be useful to work with the key-value pairs of a hash table.

Examples:

A @code{for} loop can iterate over the elements of a list.

@c ===beg===
@c for f in [log, rho, atan] do ldisp (f(1)) $
@c ===end===
@example maxima
@group
(%i1) for f in [log, rho, atan] do ldisp (f(1)) $
(%t1)                           0

(%t2)                        rho(1)

                               %pi
(%t3)                          ───
                                4
@end group
@end example

A @code{for} loop can iterate over the arguments of a general expression.

@c ===beg===
@c e: a + 1/2 + %pi*2;
@c for v in e do ldisp (v) $
@c ===end===
@example maxima
@group
(%i1) e: a + 1/2 + %pi*2;
                                      1
(%o1)                     a + 2 %pi + ─
                                      2
(%i2) for v in e do ldisp (v) $
(%t2)                           a

(%t3)                         2 %pi

                                1
(%t4)                           ─
                                2
@end group
@end example

A @code{for} loop can iterate over the key-value pairs of hash tables.
In this case, the hash table is an undeclared array.

@c ===beg===
@c (hh["foo"]: 444, hh["bar"]: 222, hh["baz"]: 777) $
@c for p in hh do ldisp (p);
@c ===end===
@example maxima
@group
(%i1) (hh["foo"]: 444, hh["bar"]: 222, hh["baz"]: 777) $

(%i2) for p in hh do ldisp (p);
(%t2)                     [[bar], 222]

(%t3)                     [[baz], 777]

(%t4)                     [[foo], 444]

(%o4)                         done
@end group
@end example

Destructuring assignments are recognized;
these may be useful to work with the key-value pairs of a hash table.

@c ===beg===
@c (hh["foo"]: 444, hh["bar"]: 222, hh["baz"]: 777) $
@c for [[k], v] in hh do disp (printf (false, "Key ~a --> value ~a", k, v));
@c ===end===
@example maxima
@group
(%i1) (hh["foo"]: 444, hh["bar"]: 222, hh["baz"]: 777) $

(%i2) for [[k], v] in hh do disp (printf (false, "Key ~a --> value ~a", k, v));
                      Key bar --> value 222

                      Key baz --> value 777

                      Key foo --> value 444

(%o2)                         done
@end group
@end example

@subsubheading Running the iterations on several cores

Writing @code{thru_parallel} in place of @code{thru}, or
@code{in_parallel} in place of @code{in}, asks for the iterations to be
evaluated on as many processor cores as are available instead of one
after another:

@example
for i: 1 thru_parallel 20 do slow_computation (i)
for x in_parallel [a, b, c] do slow_computation (x)
@end example

@code{step} may be given as usual. The value of the loop is
@code{done}, as it is for an ordinary @code{do}.

@subsubheading The iterations must not depend on each other

This is the whole of what the writer of the loop is promising, and
nothing checks it.

Iterations run at the same time, in no particular order, and one may
well finish before another starts. So no iteration may use anything
that another iteration produces --- in particular, nothing a
@emph{previous} pass through the loop computed. A loop that carries a
value forward from one pass to the next is exactly what cannot be made
parallel:

@example
/* WRONG: every pass reads what the pass before it wrote */
s: 0$
for i: 1 thru_parallel 10 do s: s + f(i)$

/* WRONG: each pass is defined in terms of the one before */
x: 1$
for i: 1 thru_parallel 10 do x: g(x)$

/* right: the passes have nothing to say to one another */
a: make_array (fixnum, 11)$
for i: 1 thru_parallel 10 do a[i]: f(i)$
@end example

The first two report no error. They quietly give an answer that depends
on which core happened to finish first, and it may differ from one run
to the next. Anything of the shape ``add it up as we go'', ``keep the
best one so far'', or ``append it to a list'' carries a value forward in
this way, however it is written.

For the same reason output from the bodies is interleaved in no defined
order, and @code{return} inside a parallel loop is an error rather than
a race, since it would name one iteration's exit out of many happening
at once.

@subsubheading Assumptions made inside a parallel body

An @mref{assume} made before the loop is in force inside it, as it would
be in a sequential loop:

@example
assume (a > 0)$
for i: 1 thru_parallel 10 do f(i, a)$
@end example

An assumption a body makes goes into the same one database a sequential
loop would have put it in, and is still there afterwards. Iterations are
therefore not insulated from one another's assumptions, and must not be
written as though they were --- an iteration whose result depends on an
assumption another iteration made gets an answer that depends on which
core reached it first. A sequential loop makes its assumptions in a
definite order, and a parallel one has no order to offer in its place.

@subsubheading What cannot be asked for

A parallel body cannot request interactive input.  @mref{read} and
@mref{readonly} signal an error before printing a prompt or consuming
input.  @mref{asksign}, @mref{askinteger}, and computations that use
them still return an answer when it can be established without asking;
if an answer from the user is needed, they signal an error instead.
Supply the necessary facts before the parallel computation, or use an
ordinary sequential computation to answer its questions interactively.

This rule also applies to @mref{parallel_makelist}, @mref{do_parallel},
and loops evaluated in parallel by @mrefdot{parallel} It holds for the
calling thread, nested parallel bodies, and the serial fallback,
including @code{parallel_threads:1}.  Thus the availability of input
does not depend on which runner evaluates a body.  Expressions which
@code{parallel} evaluates unchanged, without entering a parallel loop,
keep their ordinary input behavior.

@code{thru_parallel} and @code{in_parallel} cannot be combined with
@code{while}, @code{unless} or @code{next}, and writing that is a syntax
error rather than a loop that quietly runs on one core. Those three
decide what the next iteration is --- or whether there is one at all ---
only after a body has run, so the iterations cannot be known in advance,
and knowing them in advance is what handing work to several cores
requires.

@subsubheading When there is only one core to run on

On a Lisp without usable threads --- GCL has none, and CLISP's are not
usable --- and whenever @mref{parallel_threads} is 1, the iterations run
one after another. The answers are the same; only the time taken
differs. The same path runs when a parallel loop is reached from inside
another one that has already taken every thread the limit allows, so
nesting parallel loops is safe rather than a way to start an unbounded
number of threads.

@opencatbox{Categories:}
@category{Programming}
@closecatbox
@end deffn

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

@anchor{parallel_threads}
@defvr {Option variable} parallel_threads
Default value: 0

The greatest number of threads Maxima may use at once, counting the one
it is already running on. Parallel evaluation never starts more than
this many, however many parallel loops are nested inside one another.

0, the default, means to use every core the machine offers. 1 turns
parallel evaluation off without changing any answers: everything then
runs one iteration after another, which is also what happens on a Lisp
that has no usable threads.

The number of cores is found by the script that starts Maxima and passed
in the environment variable @code{MAXIMA_NUM_CORES}, so setting that
variable before starting Maxima fixes what @mref{parallel_cores}
reports.

@opencatbox{Categories:}
@category{Programming}
@closecatbox
@end defvr

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

@anchor{parallel_cores}
@deffn {Function} parallel_cores ()
Returns the number of threads Maxima will use at once, which is
@mref{parallel_threads} when that is positive and the number of cores
the machine reports otherwise.

@opencatbox{Categories:}
@category{Programming}
@closecatbox
@end deffn

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

@anchor{parallel_threads_available}
@deffn {Function} parallel_threads_available ()
Returns @code{false} on a Lisp where parallel evaluation falls back to
running one iteration after another --- GCL has no threads, and CLISP's
are not usable --- and @code{true} where it does not.

A @code{false} here does not mean a parallel loop will fail. It means
it will produce the same answers, taking as long as an ordinary loop.

@opencatbox{Categories:}
@category{Programming}
@closecatbox
@end deffn

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

@anchor{parallel_makelist}
@deffn {Function} parallel_makelist (@var{expr}, @var{i}, @var{i_0}, @var{i_1}, @var{step})
Constructs the same list as @mref{makelist}, evaluating the elements on
several cores. Every argument form @code{makelist} accepts is accepted
here and means the same thing, and the list is built in index order
however the work was shared out.

The elements must not depend on one another, exactly as described for
@code{thru_parallel} under @mrefdot{do} An element that reads what
another element writes gives an answer that depends on which core
finished first.

@example
(%i1) parallel_makelist (f(i), i, 1, 100)$
@end example

@code{makelist} itself is never parallel: whether the elements are
independent is something only the person writing them can know, so it
has to be asked for.

@opencatbox{Categories:}
@category{Programming}
@category{Lists}
@closecatbox
@end deffn

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

@anchor{parallel}
@deffn {Special operator} parallel (@var{expr})
Evaluates @var{expr} on several cores where it can. When @var{expr} is
a counted @code{do} loop or a @code{do} loop over a list, this is
another way of writing @code{thru_parallel} or @code{in_parallel}:

@example
parallel (for i: 1 thru 20 do slow_computation (i))
@end example

Anything else, including a loop whose iterations cannot be known before
they run, is simply evaluated as it stands. Wrapping an expression in
@code{parallel} therefore never changes what it means --- at worst it
does nothing --- but unlike @code{thru_parallel} it also gives no
warning when that is what has happened. Prefer the keywords, which
refuse a loop they cannot spread.

The iterations must be independent; see @mref{do} for what that rules
out.

@opencatbox{Categories:}
@category{Programming}
@closecatbox
@end deffn

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

@anchor{do_parallel}
@deffn {Special operator} do_parallel @
@fname{do_parallel} (@var{expr_1}, @var{expr_2}, @dots{}) @
@fname{do_parallel} ([@var{expr_1}, @var{expr_2}, @dots{}])

Evaluates the expressions on several cores at once and returns
@code{done}. The arguments may be given separately or as one list; the
two mean the same thing.

Where @mref{parallel_makelist} and a parallel @code{do} loop run
@emph{one} expression over many values, this runs @emph{many different}
expressions at the same time:

@example
do_parallel ([slow_a (x), slow_b (y), slow_c (z)])$
@end example

The arguments are not evaluated before they are handed out --- that is
the whole point, since evaluating them to pass them along would compute
every one of them, one after another, before any thread started.

The expressions must not depend on one another, for the reasons given
for @code{thru_parallel} under @mrefdot{do} They finish in no
particular order, so none of them may use what another one produces,
and their printed output is interleaved. An error in any of them
reaches the caller.

@opencatbox{Categories:}
@category{Programming}
@closecatbox
@end deffn

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

@anchor{parallel_peak_workers}
@deffn {Function} parallel_peak_workers ()
@deffnx {Function} parallel_worker_items ()
@deffnx {Function} parallel_reset_counters ()
@code{parallel_peak_workers} returns the greatest number of worker
threads that were running at once since the counters were last reset,
not counting the thread that started them.
@code{parallel_worker_items} returns how many elements or iterations
those workers evaluated. @code{parallel_reset_counters} sets both back
to zero.

These exist for testing. A parallel computation that quietly ran on one
core returns exactly the right answers, so a test that only compares
answers cannot tell the two apart and passes whatever happened;
@code{parallel_worker_items} is zero in that case and positive
otherwise, which lets such a test fail when it ought to.

@opencatbox{Categories:}
@category{Programming}
@closecatbox
@end deffn

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
@anchor{errcatch}
@deffn {Function} errcatch (@var{expr_1}, @dots{}, @var{expr_n})

Evaluates @var{expr_1}, @dots{}, @var{expr_n} one by one and
returns @code{[@var{expr_n}]} (a list) if no error occurs.  If an
error occurs in the evaluation of any argument, @code{errcatch} 
prevents the error from propagating and
returns the empty list @code{[]} without evaluating any more arguments.

@code{errcatch}
is useful in @code{batch} files where one suspects an error might occur which
would terminate the @code{batch} if the error weren't caught.

See also @mrefdot{errormsg}

@opencatbox{Categories:}
@category{Programming}
@closecatbox
@end deffn

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
@anchor{error}
@deffn  {Function} error (@var{expr_1}, @dots{}, @var{expr_n})
@deffnx {System variable} error

Evaluates and prints @var{expr_1}, @dots{}, @var{expr_n},
and then causes an error return to top level Maxima
or to the nearest enclosing @code{errcatch}.

The variable @code{error} is set to a list describing the error.
The first element of @code{error} is a format string, which merges all the
strings among the arguments @var{expr_1}, @dots{}, @var{expr_n},
and the remaining elements are the values of any non-string arguments.

@code{errormsg()} formats and prints @code{error}.
This is effectively reprinting the most recent error message.

@opencatbox{Categories:}
@category{Programming}
@closecatbox
@end deffn

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
@anchor{warning}
@deffn  {Function} warning (@var{expr_1}, @dots{}, @var{expr_n})

Evaluates and prints @var{expr_1}, @dots{}, @var{expr_n},
as a warning message that is formatted in a standard way so a maxima front-end
may be able to recognize the warning and to format it accordingly.

The function @code{warning} always returns false.

@opencatbox{Categories:}
@category{Programming}
@closecatbox
@end deffn

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
@anchor{error_size}
@defvr {Option variable} error_size
Default value: 60

@code{error_size} modifies error messages according to the size of expressions
which appear in them.  If the size of an expression (as determined by the Lisp
function @code{ERROR-SIZE}) is greater than @code{error_size}, the expression is
replaced in the message by a symbol, and the symbol is assigned the expression.
The symbols are taken from the list @mref{error_syms}.

Otherwise, the expression is smaller than @code{error_size}, and the expression
is displayed in the message.

See also @mref{error} and @mrefdot{error_syms}

Example:
@c OUTPUT GENERATED BY THE FOLLOWING
@c U: (C^D^E + B + A)/(cos(X-1) + 1)$
@c error_size: 20$
@c error ("Example expression is", U);
@c errexp1;
@c error_size: 30$
@c error ("Example expression is", U);

The size of @code{U}, as determined by @code{ERROR-SIZE}, is 24.

@example maxima
(%i1) U: (C^D^E + B + A)/(cos(X-1) + 1)$
(%i2) error_size: 20$
(%i3) error ("Example expression is", U);
Example expression is errexp1
 -- an error. To debug this try: debugmode(true);
@group
(%i4) errexp1;
                            E
                           D
                          C   + B + A
(%o4)                    --------------
                         cos(X - 1) + 1
@end group
(%i5) error_size: 30$
(%i6) error ("Example expression is", U);

                         E
                        D
                       C   + B + A
Example expression is --------------
                      cos(X - 1) + 1
 -- an error.  Quitting.  To debug this try debugmode(true);
@end example

@opencatbox{Categories:}
@category{Debugging}
@category{Display flags and variables}
@closecatbox
@end defvr

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
@anchor{errexp1}
@defvr {Global variable} errexp1
See @mrefdot{error_syms}
@end defvr

@anchor{error_syms}
@defvr {Option variable} error_syms
Default value: @code{[errexp1, errexp2, errexp3]}

In error messages, expressions larger than @mref{error_size} are replaced by
symbols, and the symbols are set to the expressions.  The symbols are taken from
the list @code{error_syms}.  The first too-large expression is replaced by
@code{error_syms[1]}, the second by @code{error_syms[2]}, and so on.

If there are more too-large expressions than there are elements of
@code{error_syms}, symbols are constructed automatically, with the @var{n}-th
symbol equivalent to @code{concat ('errexp, @var{n})}.

See also @mref{error} and @mrefdot{error_size}

@opencatbox{Categories:}
@category{Debugging}
@category{Display flags and variables}
@closecatbox
@end defvr

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
@anchor{errormsg_fn}
@deffn {Function} errormsg ()

Reprints the most recent error message.
The variable @code{error} holds the message,
and @mref{errormsg} formats and prints it.

@opencatbox{Categories:}
@category{Programming}
@closecatbox
@end deffn

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
@anchor{errormsg}
@defvr {Option variable} errormsg
Default value: @code{true}

When @code{false} the output of error messages is suppressed.

The option variable @code{errormsg} can not be set in a block to a local 
value.  The global value of @code{errormsg} is always present.

@c ===beg===
@c errormsg;
@c sin(a,b);
@c errormsg: false;
@c sin(a,b);
@c ===end===
sin(y): expected exactly 1 arguments but got 2: [a, b]
 -- an error. To debug this try: debugmode(true);
 -- an error. To debug this try: debugmode(true);
@example maxima
@group
(%i1) errormsg;
(%o1)                         true
@end group
(%i2) sin(a,b);
@group
(%i3) errormsg: false;
(%o3)                         false
@end group
(%i4) sin(a,b);
@end example

The option variable @code{errormsg} can not be set in a block to a local value.

@c ===beg===
@c f(bool):=block([errormsg:bool],
@c                      print ("value of errormsg is",errormsg))$
@c errormsg:true;
@c f(false);
@c errormsg:false;
@c f(true);
@c ===end===
@example maxima
@group
(%i1) f(bool):=block([errormsg:bool],
                     print ("value of errormsg is",errormsg))$
@end group
@group
(%i2) errormsg:true;
(%o2)                         true
@end group
@group
(%i3) f(false);
value of errormsg is false 
(%o3)                         false
@end group
@group
(%i4) errormsg:false;
(%o4)                         false
@end group
@group
(%i5) f(true);
value of errormsg is true 
(%o5)                         true
@end group
@end example

@opencatbox{Categories:}
@category{Programming}
@closecatbox
@end defvr

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
@anchor{go}
@deffn {Function} go (@var{tag})

is used within a @mref{block} to transfer control to the statement
of the block which is tagged with the argument to @code{go}.  To tag a
statement, precede it by an atomic argument as another statement in
the @code{block}.  For example:

@example
block ([x], x:1, loop, x+1, ..., go(loop), ...)
@end example

The argument to @code{go} must be the name of a tag appearing in the same
@code{block}.  One cannot use @code{go} to transfer to tag in a @code{block}
other than the one containing the @code{go}.

@opencatbox{Categories:}
@category{Programming}
@closecatbox
@end deffn

@c NEEDS CLARIFICATION, EXPANSION, EXAMPLES
@c THIS ITEM IS IMPORTANT

@c -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
@anchor{if}
@deffn {Special operator} if

Represents conditional evaluation.  Various forms of @code{if} expressions are
recognized.

@code{if @var{cond_1} then @var{expr_1} else @var{expr_0}}
eva@-lu@-ates to @var{expr_1} if @var{cond_1} evaluates to @code{true},
otherwise the expression evaluates to @var{expr_0}.

The command @code{if @var{cond_1} then @var{expr_1} elseif @var{cond_2} then
@var{expr_2} elseif ... else @var{expr_0}} evaluates to @var{expr_k} if
@var{cond_k} is @code{true} and all preceding conditions are @code{false}.  If
none of the conditions are @code{true}, the expression evaluates to
@code{expr_0}.

A trailing @code{else false} is assumed if @code{else} is missing.  That is,
the command @code{if @var{cond_1} then @var{expr_1}} is equivalent to
@code{if @var{cond_1} then @var{expr_1} else false}, and the command
@code{if @var{cond_1} then @var{expr_1} elseif ... elseif @var{cond_n} then
@var{expr_n}} is equivalent to @code{if @var{cond_1} then @var{expr_1} elseif 
... elseif @var{cond_n} then @var{expr_n} else false}.

The alternatives @var{expr_0}, @dots{}, @var{expr_n} may be any Maxima
expressions, including nested @code{if} expressions.  The alternatives are
neither simplified nor evaluated unless the corresponding condition is
@code{true}.

The conditions @var{cond_1}, @dots{}, @var{cond_n} are expressions which
potentially or actually evaluate to @code{true} or @code{false}.
When a condition does not actually evaluate to @code{true} or @code{false},
the behavior of @code{if} is governed by the global flag @code{prederror}.
When @code{prederror} is @code{true}, it is an error if any evaluated condition
does not evaluate to @code{true} or @code{false}.  Otherwise, conditions which
do not evaluate to @code{true} or @code{false} are accepted, and the result is
a conditional expression.

Among other elements, conditions may comprise relational and logical operators
as follows.

@c - SEEMS LIKE THIS TABLE WANTS TO BE IN A DISCUSSION OF PREDICATE FUNCTIONS; PRESENT LOCATION IS OK I GUESS
@c I (rtoy) would prefer to use the headitem prototype text since it's
@c easier to reason about, but currently the html output just seems to
@c ignore that and automatically makes the column width fit the widest
@c entry.  We use @columnfractions to fix that, but it's hard to know
@c a priori how wide to make each column.
